WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.070
Picture the richest person you can possibly
imagine.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.220
Is it Jeff Bezos, the super-wealthy Amazon
founder?

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.080
Or maybe Microsoft head Bill Gates?

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:36.150
No matter who you chose, their wealth will
still be peanuts next to the subject of today’s

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.150
video.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:42.360
Musa I was a man so wealthy that the true
extent of his riches are almost indescribable.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:49.050
The ruler of the Malian Empire from 1312 AD
to around 1337, Musa was a man whose life

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:50.890
was built on gold.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:54.760
As king he personally owned over half the
Old World’s known gold reserves.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:00.280
On a single trip through Cairo he once spent
so much that he caused the price of gold to

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.030
crash, pushing Egypt into an economic slump.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:08.570
Imagine if Auric Goldfinger somehow mated
with Scrooge McDuck to produce the wealthiest,

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.250
bling-iest figure in history.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.410
That was Musa I.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.720
But who really was this real-life King Midas?

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.620
Where did his empire come from, and how did
he amass so much wealth?

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.960
Born into West Africa, Musa inherited his
family’s kingdom, only to expand it into

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.299
a vast empire.

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:30.780
At a time when Europe was a land of peasants
and warfare, he managed to build a cultural

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.500
powerhouse so great, it rivaled anywhere in
the known world.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:40.820
A legendary figure in his time, often overlooked
today, this is the tale of history’s richest

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:41.950
man.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:47.630
(TITLE): The Empire of Gold

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:52.829
The story of Musa I technically starts with
his birth, in 1280 AD, to the ruling clan

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:54.350
of the Mali Empire.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:59.150
But it really starts way before that, toward
the beginning of the 13th Century, with a

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.609
very different kind of birth:

00:02:01.609 --> 00:02:03.070
The birth of an empire.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:08.070
In 1230 - around 50 years before Musa was
born - West Africa’s dominant state was

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.070
the Ghanan Empire.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:10.070
((NOTE FOR EDITORS: The Ghana Empire didn’t
include modern Ghana, so let’s not use any

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:11.070
images of today’s Ghana to illustrate this
bit))

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.140
But we only mean “dominant” in terms of
size.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:16.640
Founded back in the 6th Century, Ghana was
by now the sick man of Africa, an empire that

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.569
was frail and tottering on the very edges
of death’s door.

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:24.819
It would be Musa’s great uncle who would
shove old man Ghana over the threshold.

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:28.730
Today, little is known about the early life
of Sundiata Keita.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:33.640
Most of the stories about him come down via
oral traditions, and seem to be a mix of fact

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:34.640
and legend.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.250
What we do know is that Sundiata Keita - a
name which means “lion prince” - forged

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:45.610
an alliance of tribal leaders and Arab merchants
in the 1230s with one aim: establishing a

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.280
new, dominant power in West Africa.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.140
This was easier said than done.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:55.390
Although Ghana was disintegrating, there was
already a rival poised to take its place.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:01.830
The Kingdom of Sosso was rising, primed to
dominate the region just as Ghana once had.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:06.830
This meant that any attempt by Sundiata to
kill the dying empire might result in someone

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.650
else getting all its riches.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:12.350
So Sundiata decided he’d just have to kill
them both.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:17.590
In 1235, Sundiata crushed the armies of Sosso
at the epic Battle of Krina.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:22.340
That done, he pivoted and attacked Ghana,
capturing its capital in 1240.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:27.061
In the aftermath of Ghana’s fall, Sundiata
burned its capital to the ground, ending over

00:03:27.061 --> 00:03:29.230
six centuries of history.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:33.570
From the ashes, he built a new superpower
the likes of which Africa had never seen.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:38.810
The basic structure of Sundiata’s new empire
was an assembly of West African warriors and

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:42.880
Arabic traders, subordinate to the supreme
monarch: i.e.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:43.880
Sundiata.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.730
And we mean subordinate.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.950
In this new empire, Sundiata’s word was
final.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.470
Only he was allowed to own gold nuggets, and
every slave in his dominions was personally

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.700
loyal to him and him alone.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:00.290
And, while the new empire’s religion was
nominally Islam, it was actually closer to

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.650
a God-like veneration of its leader.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.150
The name “Mali” even means “the place
where the king lives.”

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:11.150
While this might sound like Sundiata is setting
himself up to become Kim Jong-Un’s skinnier,

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.360
13th Century cousin, that’s not what happened.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:20.620
While Sundiata was certainly egomaniacal,
he knew his new empire might crumble if he

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.290
went marching through Africa, demanding everyone
worship him.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:29.100
Instead, he set about building a network of
alliances that would hold his empire together.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.789
You know today how the EU is technically run
out of Brussels, but is really a connection

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:37.130
of autonomous states that often just do their
own thing?

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:41.220
Well, in a very basic sense, that was Sundiata’s
Mali.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:46.330
A huge degree of autonomy was granted to its
various regions, with Sundiata leaving them

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:51.010
alone so long as they kept passing on gold,
slaves, and taxes.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:57.210
This hands-off approach worked, allowing Sundiata
to bring useful trade routes under his control.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:02.790
By the time the Lion Prince kicked the bucket
around 1255, the Malian Empire was the largest

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.320
Africa had ever seen.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.160
Yet even this superstate would only be a footnote
to what came next.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:14.490
In just a few short decades, Musa was gonna
take his great uncle’s already pretty-decent

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:18.130
empire, and make it truly great.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:23.190
(TITLE): The Vanished King

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:28.010
By the time Musa was born in 1280, the might
of Mali was well established.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:32.930
Sundiata’s empire controlled trade on both
the Niger and Sankarani Rivers, with its now

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:37.880
long-lost capital of Niani standing on the
plains beside the latter.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:42.310
Mali also held an excellent strategic position
between the gold fields of West Africa and

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:48.440
the salt-trading caliphates of the north,
allowing it to tax the heck outta both commodities.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:53.060
In fact, the empire had amassed so much gold
that it traded it as far afield as Venice

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.080
and the Kingdom of Castille.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.580
And this wealth really did trickle down to
the empire’s subjects.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:02.550
Travellers at the time noted that there was
food in every village, that the justice system

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:05.420
worked, and that travel between regions was
easy.

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.900
While that might sound kinda “yeah.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:11.930
And?”, just remember that Europe at this
time is full of rampaging Mongols killing

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:16.740
everything in sight, while England is dividing
its time between war with the Scots and expelling

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:17.970
the Jews.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.960
Mali in the 13th Century, guys.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:21.440
It’s the place to be.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.600
Still, we don’t want to give the impression
that Mali was the sole power in West Africa.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.080
At this stage, the river port of Timbuktu
was still the most powerful and cosmopolitan

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:35.520
city for hundreds of kilometers, where everything
from ivory to textiles to spices were traded.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:41.270
You won’t be surprised to hear that this
made the rulers of Mali all sorts of jealous.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.400
But what were they to do?

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:48.450
After Sundiata’s death, Mali had passed
onto a succession of rulers best described

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:53.900
as being somewhere between mediocre and solid-gold
dumbasses.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:57.770
If the Empire of Mali wanted to go down in
history as more than just the product of a

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:02.170
single man, it was gonna have to start producing
some decent rulers.

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:03.530
Like, now.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:07.370
Luckily, it’s greatest ruler was already
waiting in the wings.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:12.900
OK, so, having established the world he lived
in, we can finally start talking about the

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.090
man himself.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:16.940
Musa was a guy who was never destined to be
king.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:23.130
In the 1300s, the throne actually went to
his brother, Mansa Abu-Bakr II; a guy with

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.390
vision to rival Sundiata.

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:30.570
Unfortunately, that vision was of how to write
himself out the history books in the weirdest

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.940
way possible.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:36.260
From the moment he took the throne, Abu-Bakr
was obsessed with the sea.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:41.570
Mali’s most-westernly point reached the
Atlantic, and it’s said Abu-Bakr spent years

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:45.880
gazing at the ocean, trying to figure out
what lay on the other side of it.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:50.860
Eventually, this obsession became a plan,
which in turn became a bizarre reality.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:55.471
In 1312, Abu-Bakr appointed Musa regent to
reign in his name.

00:07:55.471 --> 00:08:00.699
Then he got into a ship at the head of a 2,000-strong
fleet and, accompanied by tens of thousands

00:08:00.699 --> 00:08:04.300
of soldiers, women, and slaves, set sail for
the horizon.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:07.510
None of those 2,000 ships was ever seen again.

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:12.910
Today, there are a very small number of academics
who think Abu-Bakr II sailed clear to the

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:17.410
New World; that he initiated Africa’s first
contact with the Americas.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:21.660
But there is no archeological evidence of
this, and it seems unlikely.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:26.070
Although the Malians used ships, they weren’t
explorers, preferring instead to navigate

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.440
up rivers and close to coastline.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:34.169
When Abu-Bakr vanished over the horizon, it’s
almost certain that the only thing he discovered

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:37.150
was how it feels to die at sea.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:41.529
Back in Mali, Musa waited a few months for
his brother to return, scanning the horizons

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:43.419
for signs of the fleet.

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:48.410
When he didn’t, Musa had himself officially
crowned Mansa Musa I.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.320
It was an opportunistic powergrab, nothing
more.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:54.220
A brother trying to outshine his older sibling.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:09:31.509
It was also 
the start

00:09:31.509 --> 00:09:55.459
of Mali’s rise to greatness.

00:09:55.459 --> 00:10:13.930
(TITLE): The Age of Expansion

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:34.769
Remember how

00:10:34.769 --> 00:10:38.480
we said Sundiata’s Mali was the biggest
empire Africa had ever seen?

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:42.670
Well, Musa wouldn’t be content with just
holding African records.

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:46.170
He wanted to compete with the entire known
world.

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:51.000
Not long after assuming the throne, Musa raised
an army of 100,000 men and 10,000 horses.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:55.959
He then headed out and conquered the living
crap outta everything.

00:10:55.959 --> 00:10:59.529
Over the next decade or so, Musa managed to
double Mali’s size.

00:10:59.529 --> 00:11:04.360
Under his watch, the empire grew to include
basically all of modern-day Senegal and The

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:09.590
Gambia, plus parts of Niger, Mauritania, Mali,
Burkina Faso, Guinea-Bissau, Guinea and the

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:10.890
Ivory Coast.

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:17.389
By the time he was done, Mali stretched over
3,200km east to west - or roughly the distance

00:11:17.389 --> 00:11:19.560
from New York City to New Mexico.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.680
At that point, the only larger empire on Earth
belonged to the Mongols.

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:29.550
To consolidate his new lands, Musa divided
his empire into 14 provinces, and placed each

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:32.149
under the control of a local farba.

00:11:32.149 --> 00:11:36.949
Like Sundiata, Musa gave his farbas a huge
degree of autonomy, allowing them to preserve

00:11:36.949 --> 00:11:41.110
linguistic, religious, and ethnic identities
in the newly-conquered territories.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:47.380
But, like Sundiata, he also made sure that
the farbas’ autonomy came at a price: gold.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:51.199
As much gold as they could get their grubby
hands on.

00:11:51.199 --> 00:11:54.990
By now, Mali’s borders encompassed half
the world’s known gold reserves.

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:58.300
And Musa claimed this all for himself.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:03.699
Along with taxes, tariffs, and slaves, he
decreed that all gold not in the form of dust

00:12:03.699 --> 00:12:06.230
should go to him and him alone.

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:09.550
Throughout history, there have been some incomprehensibly
wealthy people.

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:14.910
The Roman Emperor Augustus would be worth
over 4.6 trillion dollars in today’s money.

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:20.519
During his reign, the Chinese emperor Shenzong
of Song controlled up to 25% of global GDP.

00:12:20.519 --> 00:12:26.540
But not even these two combined could hold
a candle to Musa I’s gold-stuffed pantaloons.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:32.720
Yet, despite being so rich he could buy and
sell Mr. Burns ten million times over, Musa

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:35.199
was almost completely anonymous.

00:12:35.199 --> 00:12:40.259
Outside of West Africa, the Malian Empire
was nearly unknown, except to traders in a

00:12:40.259 --> 00:12:42.360
handful of European and Arabic cities.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:47.260
But if the rest of the world still thought
of West Africa as nothing but jungle, they

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.379
were about to get a rude awakening.

00:12:49.379 --> 00:12:53.920
So, we haven’t made a big deal out of it
so far, but you might recall us mentioning

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:55.740
that Mali’s official religion was Islam.

00:12:55.740 --> 00:13:00.949
Well, just like now, Islam in the 14th Century
was super-hot on the Hajj - the idea that

00:13:00.949 --> 00:13:04.209
every Muslim should go to Mecca at least once
in their lives.

00:13:04.209 --> 00:13:08.959
Malian kings before Musa had done so, but
they tended to travel in small groups befitting

00:13:08.959 --> 00:13:09.959
pilgrims.

00:13:09.959 --> 00:13:13.410
In 1324, Musa decided it was time to do his
Hajj.

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:18.639
But he was gonna travel in a group befitting
not a pilgrim, but a king.

00:13:18.639 --> 00:13:19.949
The king.

00:13:19.949 --> 00:13:22.490
The richest guy who ever lived.

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:25.629
It was a decision that would cause a sensation.

00:13:25.629 --> 00:13:30.939
In July that year, Musa set off from his empire
at the head of a caravan of 60,000 men.

00:13:30.939 --> 00:13:35.910
There were court officials, soldiers, traditional
singers known as griots, merchants, camel

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:36.910
drivers, women.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:40.889
There were 12,000 slaves, and tens of thousands
of sheep for food.

00:13:40.889 --> 00:13:43.029
A hundred camels brought up the rear.

00:13:43.029 --> 00:13:47.319
And every single living thing was absolutely
covered in gold.

00:13:47.319 --> 00:13:53.760
Imagine the archetype of a mid-90s West Coast
rapper, barely able to move beneath his bling.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:58.680
Well that guy would’ve looked underdressed
next to even Musa’s lowliest servant.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.850
Everyone was wearing gold brocade and luxury
Persian silk.

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:06.389
The first 500 slaves additionally each carried
3 kilos of solid gold.

00:14:06.389 --> 00:14:10.980
Each of the 100 camels was loaded down with
135 kilos of gold.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:13.739
Thousands of horses hauled the finest textiles
in Africa.

00:14:13.739 --> 00:14:18.459
It was a cavalcade of wealth, a procession
of such riches that it probably looked like

00:14:18.459 --> 00:14:19.999
a mobile Fort Knox.

00:14:19.999 --> 00:14:25.209
And when word of it finally reached Arabia
and Europe, it was going to upend everything

00:14:25.209 --> 00:14:28.779
people thought they knew.

00:14:28.779 --> 00:14:32.970
(TITLE): Gold Fever

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:37.240
Although Musa was traveling at the head of
a caravan made entirely of bling, that didn’t

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:38.779
mean his mission was totally peaceful.

00:14:38.779 --> 00:14:43.470
En-route to Mecca, he detoured to the Songhai
Kingdom and conquered it, adding several hundred

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.189
km2 to his empire.

00:14:45.189 --> 00:14:46.189
Y’know.

00:14:46.189 --> 00:14:47.189
As you do.

00:14:47.189 --> 00:14:51.120
The conquest finally brought Timbuktu into
Mali’s fold, adding that city’s immense

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:52.790
wealth to Musa’s pocketbook.

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:56.779
Still, this bit of recreational conquering
was just a sideshow.

00:14:56.779 --> 00:15:01.600
What really caused a storm was when Musa and
his entourage arrived in Cairo.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:06.689
In 1324, Cairo was meant to be the richest
city in Africa, ruled over by the great Sultan

00:15:06.689 --> 00:15:11.110
al-Malik al-Nasir, a dude who was himself
no stranger to wealth.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:14.879
But when Musa’s entourage came marching
in, waving more gold than anyone had ever

00:15:14.879 --> 00:15:19.799
seen, even the Sultan had to stoop to pick
his jaw up off the ground.

00:15:19.799 --> 00:15:25.149
Musa’s first act was to send the Sultan
50,000 gold dinars simply as a way of saying

00:15:25.149 --> 00:15:29.779
“hi” - effectively handing him over 210
kilos of gold.

00:15:29.779 --> 00:15:34.449
Just to make sure we’re all clear on how
crazy this is, a kilo of gold today retails

00:15:34.449 --> 00:15:36.350
for roughly $50,000.

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:42.549
And Musa had handed over 210 to the Sultan
while basically saying “oh this?

00:15:42.549 --> 00:15:44.290
Just a little something to say thanks.

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:46.100
Great city you got here, by the way!”

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:49.720
But while you or I would probably be so excited
to receive so much gold that a little bit

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:53.980
of pee might squirt out, in Cairo it almost
caused a major diplomatic incident.

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:58.949
After receiving Musa’s gift, Sultan al-Malik
al-Nasir demanded the Malian king come over

00:15:58.949 --> 00:15:59.949
to meet him.

00:15:59.949 --> 00:16:03.610
But since this would involve Musa having to
kiss the ground before the Sultan as a mark

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:04.749
of respect, Musa refused.

00:16:04.749 --> 00:16:11.579
This standoff of “no, I’m more important!”
actually got so heated that it briefly looked

00:16:11.579 --> 00:16:13.720
like a riot was gonna break out in Cairo.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:16.050
But, in the end, Musa swallowed his pride.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:17.779
He went to the Sultan and kissed the ground.

00:16:17.779 --> 00:16:22.799
In return, the Sultan opened Cairo to Musa
as his personal playground.

00:16:22.799 --> 00:16:27.399
The Malians wound up staying three months
in the city, living in a vast palace the Sultan

00:16:27.399 --> 00:16:28.620
put aside for them.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:32.540
Every day, Musa would go out and wander this
city of nearly one million souls.

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:36.999
And every day, he would hand out his gold
like the nuggets were tic tacs.

00:16:36.999 --> 00:16:40.589
At the same time, his entourage were going
through the markets, buying all the stuff

00:16:40.589 --> 00:16:42.309
they couldn’t get at home.

00:16:42.309 --> 00:16:46.769
Seeing all these super-rich Malians, the Egyptian
merchants marked up their prices.

00:16:46.769 --> 00:16:49.079
When the Malians paid regardless, they marked
them up again.

00:16:49.079 --> 00:16:50.079
And again.

00:16:50.079 --> 00:16:51.079
And again.

00:16:51.079 --> 00:16:52.779
Soon, Cairo was swimming in gold.

00:16:52.779 --> 00:16:56.549
Stories of Musa’s largesse were fanning
out across the trade routes, bringing more

00:16:56.549 --> 00:16:57.949
and more people to the city.

00:16:57.949 --> 00:17:00.389
Unfortunately, Musa was maybe a little too
generous.

00:17:00.389 --> 00:17:05.089
You may have heard the story about how, during
the Spanish conquest of Latin America, the

00:17:05.089 --> 00:17:09.210
conquistadors brought so much gold back to
Spain that the price crashed.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:14.730
Well, what hundreds of conquistadors did to
Spain, Musa did to Cairo all by himself.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:20.300
His generosity so flooded the market that
over 20% was wiped off the value of gold.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:25.170
The result was an economic slump that Cairo
wouldn’t recover from for 12 damn years.

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:28.899
But it’s doubtful Musa even noticed the
chaos he caused.

00:17:28.899 --> 00:17:32.860
After three months, his entourage marched
out of Cairo and on to Mecca, leaving only

00:17:32.860 --> 00:17:36.510
glittering memories and a gold-induced hangover.

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:40.899
Although Cairo would ultimately suffer from
its flooded gold market, the crash made Musa’s

00:17:40.899 --> 00:17:41.899
reputation.

00:17:41.899 --> 00:17:45.690
In Europe, people finally started hearing
about this king who was so wealthy his supply

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:47.429
of gold never ran out.

00:17:47.429 --> 00:17:53.309
From that point on, Musa’s name would pass
into legend.

00:17:53.309 --> 00:17:57.010
(TITLE): A Brand New Culture

00:17:57.010 --> 00:18:01.590
When Musa finally reached Arabia, he kept
right on throwing his gold about like some

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:03.529
kinda Vegas high roller.

00:18:03.529 --> 00:18:08.980
He bought up land, splashed cash on houses
for Malians to stay in on future pilgrimages.

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:13.399
But he also took time out from flooding the
gold market to check out Mecca’s sights:

00:18:13.399 --> 00:18:16.970
its mosques and minaret; its schools and libraries.

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:19.340
Evidently, Musa liked what he saw.

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:25.200
When the Malians finally left Arabia, in 1325,
Musa didn’t just leave a good Muslim.

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:27.650
He left as a good Muslim with a plan.

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:32.649
A plan to turn the Malian Empire into a cultural
powerhouse to rival Mecca.

00:18:32.649 --> 00:18:37.110
And while his insane wealth may be why everyone
remembers him today, it was this decision

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:40.560
more than anything which would lead to his
lasting legacy.

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:45.070
The first thing Musa did was use his vast
wealth to tempt as many Islamic scholars from

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:46.779
Mecca as he could find.

00:18:46.779 --> 00:18:50.419
The group of intellectuals he netted even
included direct descendants of the Prophet

00:18:50.419 --> 00:18:54.230
Mohammed, earning the Malian king serious
bragging rights.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:57.890
That done, Musa set off in his caravan and
headed back to Cairo.

00:18:57.890 --> 00:19:02.679
The Egyptian city was still suffering the
aftereffects of his last visit, but Musa wasn’t

00:19:02.679 --> 00:19:04.620
there to cause another economic slump.

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:07.370
No, he was back for one man.

00:19:07.370 --> 00:19:09.970
One man, in a city of almost a million.

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:12.690
A man known as Abu Ishak al-Tuedjin.

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:18.580
A poet and architect from Moorish Spain, al-Tuedjin
was something of a Cairo celebrity.

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:23.519
And there he might have remained, had Musa
not dumped 200 kilos of gold at his feet,

00:19:23.519 --> 00:19:28.460
and declared the architect could keep all
that, plus dozens of slaves, plus a swathe

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:32.640
of land along the Niger River, if he only
came to work for Mali.

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.690
When Musa’s caravan left Cairo shortly after,
Abu Ishak al-Tuedjin went with him.

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:36.690
It was on the journey home that Musa gave
al-Tuedjin his mission.

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:40.409
In the conquered kingdom of Songhai, there
lay a city of immense wealth known as Timbuktu.

00:19:40.409 --> 00:19:45.110
Al-Tuedjin’s job was to take that city,
and transform it into a glistening pearl on

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:48.639
the River, a town to rival Mecca in its grandeur.

00:19:48.639 --> 00:19:51.720
To make that happen, he’d first have to
build the greatest mosque Africa had ever

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:52.720
seen.

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:56.860
Today, the Djinguereber Mosque is recognized
as an architectural masterpiece.

00:19:56.860 --> 00:20:01.269
Protected by UNESCO, it’s known for its
soaring mud brick architecture with exposed

00:20:01.269 --> 00:20:04.380
wooden beams, and minarets that look like
pyramids.

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:08.950
Built by adding layer upon layer of wet earth
until it finally took shape, it has absolutely

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:13.400
nothing in common with the grand white stone
mosques of Morocco or Turkey.

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:19.000
And yet, it’s this simple style and use
of cheap materials that make it so beautiful.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:23.600
Employing tradesmen from as far away as Yemen,
al-Tuedjin built a mosque using the same techniques

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:27.549
and materials as the poor in Timbuktu used
to build their homes.

00:20:27.549 --> 00:20:32.080
In doing so, he created a building both sacred
and accessible; a building that continues

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:34.480
to function as a mosque to this very day.

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:39.110
It probably says something that, even when
al-Qaeda took over Timbuktu in 2012, and set

00:20:39.110 --> 00:20:43.220
about blowing up all traces of its Malian
past, they still couldn’t bring themselves

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:45.460
to destroy al-Tuedjin’s mosque.

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:49.690
But the Djinguereber Mosque wasn’t the only
thing Musa brought to Timbuktu.

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:52.830
Under his watch, the city was rejuvenated.

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:57.289
Already a wealthy port, it soon became peppered
with libraries, schools, and the foundations

00:20:57.289 --> 00:20:58.289
of universities.

00:20:58.289 --> 00:21:04.149
Musa threw gold at the arts, at culture, and
soon 14th Century Timbuktu was what Paris

00:21:04.149 --> 00:21:05.960
was to the 19th Century.

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:11.710
In short, this great city in this great empire
became the focal point of an entire continent.

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:16.480
While it would never quite achieve Musa’s
dream of rivaling Mecca, it sure as Hell came

00:21:16.480 --> 00:21:17.480
close.

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:20.759
Unfortunately, this greatness wasn’t destined
to last.

00:21:20.759 --> 00:21:26.400
In its lifetime, the Malian Empire never really
achieved greatness in and of itself.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:31.479
Instead, it just had the luck to be blessed
with two great rulers - Sundiata and Musa

00:21:31.479 --> 00:21:35.059
- who separately managed to take it to its
height.

00:21:35.059 --> 00:21:38.350
When Sundiata finally died, Mali had stagnated.

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:39.580
But when Musa died…

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:45.640
...it would be “so long, Mali.”

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:49.580
(TITLE): Decline and Fall

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:51.700
In 1337, Musa I passed away of natural causes.

00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:56.580
He left behind an empire at its height, and
a throne which would make anyone who sat on

00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:59.250
it the richest person in existence.

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:00.919
And therein lay the problem.

00:22:00.919 --> 00:22:04.370
No-one could decide who got to sit on that
throne.

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:08.900
Way back when Sundiata established his empire,
he hadn’t written into law the firstborns’

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:10.750
right of succession.

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:13.970
This was fine when everyone got on and agreed
who should be king.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:18.710
But when they didn’t, it had only one - painfully
obvious - outcome.

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:19.740
Civil war.

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:23.400
In the wake of Musa’s death, the Malian
Empire began its depressing decline.

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:28.769
Barely was the great Mansa in the ground than
various provinces split off, or declared themselves

00:22:28.769 --> 00:22:32.350
in rebellion, or got sucked into squabbles
between would-be rulers.

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:38.029
While Mali itself would survive over another
century, it never again covered such an extent

00:22:38.029 --> 00:22:39.640
as in Musa’s day.

00:22:39.640 --> 00:22:42.830
Never again made its ruler quite so wealthy.

00:22:42.830 --> 00:22:45.309
The final collapse came in the mid-15th Century.

00:22:45.309 --> 00:22:49.610
New gold fields were discovered while, at
the same time, new trade routes opened up

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:54.019
across Africa, robbing Mali of both precious
metals and taxes.

00:22:54.019 --> 00:22:59.240
By 1464, the once-subordinate Songhai Kingdom
had not just declared independence, but grown

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:04.530
so strong that its rulers were able to attack
and overrun what remained of Mali, absorbing

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:06.330
its riches into their own kingdom.

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:11.039
And, because the Songhai were apparently total
dicks, their conquest included ransacking

00:23:11.039 --> 00:23:16.600
Timbuktu five separate times, until almost
nothing remained of Musa’s great city.

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:22.120
A mere 200 years after it had first come into
existence, the story of Mali was at last over.

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:24.000
But not the story of Musa.

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:29.980
Back in 1375, a Spanish geographer had heard
tales of Musa’s stay in Cairo, and been

00:23:29.980 --> 00:23:36.299
so inspired by the gold king that he created
history’s first serious map of West Africa.

00:23:36.299 --> 00:23:41.970
That map had included an image of Musa, clutching
a vast gold nugget, looking like a figure

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:43.220
from fantasy.

00:23:43.220 --> 00:23:47.450
And it would be this image, more than anything
else, that would inspire the generations to

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:48.450
come.

00:23:48.450 --> 00:23:53.539
Right up until the 18th Century, Timbuktu
was the El Dorado of Africa; a place of legend

00:23:53.539 --> 00:23:57.019
where the streets were meant to be paved with
gold.

00:23:57.019 --> 00:24:02.230
Tales of this semi-mystical city inspired
many to explore the Gold Coast, transforming

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:04.149
European understanding of Africa.

00:24:04.149 --> 00:24:09.059
And while the Timbuktu they finally found
wasn’t quite the city of myth, it was still

00:24:09.059 --> 00:24:13.759
home to one of the greatest examples of religious
architecture in history.

00:24:13.759 --> 00:24:18.279
The story of Musa I, then, is certainly a
tale of unimaginable riches.

00:24:18.279 --> 00:24:21.759
I mean, this guy’s gold crashed an entire
economy!

00:24:21.759 --> 00:24:26.100
But it’s also the tale of an African king
who built an empire that could rival any in

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:27.100
Europe or Asia.

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:32.639
Of a man who patronized learning and culture
in a region those elsewhere wrote off as a

00:24:32.639 --> 00:24:33.960
place of savages.

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:39.620
He might be semi-forgotten now, but Musa I
of Mali remains one of history’s great leaders.

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:44.000
Next time you see modern billionaires like
Jeff Bezos, Warren Buffet, or Donald Trump

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:49.889
on TV, just remember the king who was richer
than them all… and the great African city

00:24:49.889 --> 00:25:02.789
he used those riches to build.

